Reasoning:

The request is a clean interannual forcing contrast: how does water partitioning (ET vs. surface runoff vs. sub-surface drainage, plus snow storage) differ between a wet year (1993) and a dry year (2002) in the Salt River headwaters. The primary lever is the FORCING PERIOD — exactly what the stack supports. Arizona is inside CONUS, so NLDAS-2 (0.125°) is available and delivers year-specific and location-specific precipitation/temperature, including some orographic signal across the basin's very large relief (~2400 m).

Two goals: (1) mechanistic — quantify the wet/dry partitioning shift and how it varies along the elevation/climate gradient (snow-dominated high country vs. semi-arid valleys); (2) validation — compare to observations. The wet/dry contrast is a per-column TREATMENT (each column is run for both years with spin-up), not a separate set of columns.

Elevation is a candidate driver but must be feasibility-checked. NLDAS DOES vary precip/temperature with location/elevation at 12 km, so a coarse elevation-band stratification is meaningful. BUT sub-12 km terrain and true lapse-rate downscaling are unresolved, and high ridgetop snow will be underestimated — so I treat elevation as PARTIALLY resolved and stratify by it while flagging the limit.

Validation is the weak point. The only in-domain observation is USGS stream gauges, but single-column ELM has NO lateral routing — there is no native integrated streamflow to compare against a gauge. That requires an aggregation/routing step that is not implemented. SNOTEL is sparse/missing, so co-located SWE validation isn't reliable. I therefore flag streamflow validation as requiring a routing capability, and offer only a basin-aggregated runoff-vs-gauge sanity check (labelled aggregated, not co-located).

Because a wet/dry contrast depends on comparable initial states (soil moisture, snow, water table), each column needs a WTD-informed warm start plus a couple of spin-up years so the contrast reflects meteorology, not cold-start artifacts.

Design: stratify by 3 elevation/climate bands × dominant soil, ~2 columns per band (one valley/riparian oversample), ≈7 exploratory columns, each run for 1993 and 2002. Small and defensible.

